Part I. Introduction and Russia\u8217?s culture as a dialogue of peoples\par
From the first part of the scan (approximate pages 1\u8211?30)\par
An address to fifth-graders and the textbook\u8217?s symbols\par
The cover carries the author\u8217?s name, Natalya Fyodorovna Vinogradova, and the title of the course. A collage of church domes, a Buddhist stupa, the Star of David, Islamic ornament, and portraits states the book\u8217?s idea at once: Russia is a country of many peoples and several traditional religions.\par
The title page says this is a Grade 5 textbook, the second stereotyped edition from the Prosveshcheniye publishing house (2022), approved by the Ministry of Education. The book continues what children studied in Grade 4 in \u8220?Foundations of Religious Cultures and Secular Ethics.\u8221? It helps them see how Russian culture took shape and where folk customs and religious traditions in it come from.\par
In the address to fifth-graders the author promises a conversation about patriotism and humanism, about the family, about care for children and older people, about the country\u8217?s past, and about people whose work raised its spiritual culture. The lessons are not meant as rote learning: children debate, solve logic problems, act out everyday situations, do projects, and learn to look at themselves \u8212? at their interests, dreams, and habits.\par
The textbook then explains its icons. An orange question mark means \u8220?you already know this.\u8221? Speech bubbles invite discussion. A microphone means \u8220?say what you think.\u8221? Small figures mean pair or group work. An exclamation mark marks a conclusion. A check mark is independent work; a star is a harder task; a little suitcase is homework. A historian\u8217?s quill leads into the past. A cross, the Star of David, the dharma wheel, and a crescent mark texts on Orthodoxy, Judaism, Buddhism, and Islam. A glossary word is highlighted; an open book means a project.\par
The introduction begins by recalling Grade 4 and asking why a citizen should know the culture of his or her country. For discussion the book gives a remark by Nikolai Dobrolyubov: in a decent person patriotism is the wish to work for the good of the homeland and to do good as much as possible and as well as possible. The lesson\u8217?s conclusion: labor shows how a person relates to society, and a contribution to the people\u8217?s culture is one form of love for the Fatherland.\par
What culture is\par
The first large theme is the idea of culture itself. A \u8220?historian\u8221? with a magic pen takes the reader back to prehistoric times. Even then people built dwellings, made tools, sewed clothing from hides, wove baskets, and hollowed out dishes. That is the material side of life. Beside it a spiritual side was born: speech, the training of endurance and discipline, the first beliefs. Fetishism is faith in the power of protective objects. Totemism is faith in kinship with animals. Scenes of hunting and ritual survive on rock and bone; soot and ochre served as paint. From ritual dances myths grew in time, and later still fairy tales and proverbs.\par
The textbook asks students not to look down on prehistory. Culture is a level of development of both society and the individual: mind, morality, and a sense of beauty embodied in things and in ideas. Values accumulate from age to age.\par
Material culture is what people make from nature in order to live: a house, clothing, tools. Spiritual culture is science, art, literature, morals, law, and politics. The boundary is conventional: a temple is stone and a holy place at once; a book is paper and thought.\par
The sources of culture\u8217?s growth are shown in a diagram: the appearance of language and literacy; scientific discoveries; changes in the family; tools, crafts, and production; paganism and then monotheistic religions; folklore and folk traditions; original creative work, art, and literature. A broadly educated person takes an interest in the culture of more than his or her own people. That does not weaken love of the homeland; it makes that love deeper and kinder toward neighbors.\par
Then the children consider whom to call a cultured person. One says: someone who does not shout and knows how to listen. Another: someone who reads a great deal. Another: someone who acts according to conscience. Another recalls the culture of scholars\u8217? mental work. The argument ends with a fair guess: they are all right. A person\u8217?s culture is made of intellectual, speech, moral, and emotional sides, and of a culture of doing.\par
Russia\u8217?s culture as a dialogue among the cultures of different peoples\par
Russian culture is shown not as one people\u8217?s monologue but as a long conversation among many. An example from Slavic antiquity is knotted writing. In fairy tales Baba Yaga gives a ball of thread: folklore specialists see a guidebook in it, where a knot equals a word. Similar signs are found in pagan burials. In the Karelian-Finnish Kalevala a singer winds songs into a ball and hides them in a casket. Echoes live in speech: \u8220?a knot for memory,\u8221? the \u8220?tying\u8221? and \u8220?untying\u8221? of a plot, \u8220?speaking in a tangle.\u8221?\par
Another turn of the same theme is the birth of printing. Manuscripts were copied mainly by monks, and errors distorted the meaning. Ivan the Terrible ordered a Printing Yard built at treasury expense (around 1553). On 1 March 1564 Ivan Fyodorov finished the Apostol \u8212? the first precisely dated Russian printed book, about two thousand copies, text in two colors, with an afterword by the master. Soon Fyodorov and Pyotr Mstislavets left Moscow: envious people accused them of \u8220?heresy.\u8221? Later they opened a press in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. The printed word made culture more accessible and more resistant to distortion.\par
The dialogue of cultures is also heard in oral epic. The Karelian rune-singer Arhippa Perttunen (1769\u8211?1841), son of peasant singers from Arkhangelsk Governorate, astonished listeners with his manner of performance. The Finnish collector Elias L\u246?nnrot recorded runes from him for more than two days and included them in the Kalevala. In all, more than six thousand lines of verse were preserved from Perttunen; the whole family continued the song tradition. The textbook asks: is such mastery needed only by family and neighbors \u8212? or does it become the country\u8217?s heritage?\par
Folk crafts are part of the shared culture as well. Yakut masters are famous for carving in wood and bone, chasing in silver, copper, and gold, embroidery, fur mosaic, and braiding from horsehair. In the North Caucasus live traditions of weapons, silver, ceramics, toys, and carpets \u8212? in Ossetia, Kabardino-Balkaria, Dagestan, Chechnya, Ingushetia, and Adygea. In Tatar art ancient ideals of beauty and the norms of Islam are strong: people and animals are not depicted, but floral ornament covers clothing, leather mosaic on footwear, dishes, towels, and rugs. In each case climate, daily life, faith, and people\u8217?s inventiveness can be seen.\par
The human being as creator and bearer of culture\par
The next section answers why some names remain in history and others disappear. A country\u8217?s culture is the fruit of the labor of many peoples and gifted people. Below are brief portraits as the textbook presents them, in other words.\par
Mikhail Lomonosov (1711\u8211?1765) was a peasant from the North and an encyclopedist: chemistry and physics, geology, history, poetry, the Russian language. He learned to read and write from a sexton faster than the teacher himself. After his mother\u8217?s death life at home became unbearable, and at nineteen he left for Moscow with a fish convoy. To enter the Slavic Greek Latin Academy he called himself a nobleman\u8217?s son \u8212? otherwise the way was closed. The best students were sent to Germany. Later he developed molecular-kinetic ideas about heat, built instruments, worked in mosaic of colored glass, and drafted the plan for Moscow University, which now bears his name. The textbook asks: would talent alone have been enough without persistence?\par
Ivan Kulibin (1735\u8211?1818) was a mechanic from a merchant background. He taught himself mechanics and kept a watch workshop. For three years he made egg-shaped pocket watches with music and tiny figures and presented them to Catherine II. The empress put him in charge of the workshop of the Academy of Sciences. He designed a single-arch wooden bridge over the Neva, a lifting chair, a pedal carriage, prostheses, and vessels with machine engines. The authorities often would not fund his serious designs, but the people remembered \u8220?Kulibin\u8221? as a name for any inventor.\par
Yuri Rytkheu (1930\u8211?2008) was the first writer of the Chukchi people to become known worldwide. His given name means \u8220?unknown\u8221?; he took a Russian first name and patronymic later. He sailed as a seaman, went with geologists, worked as a stevedore \u8212? and all of it later entered his prose. He began publishing as a teenager. At university he translated Pushkin, Tolstoy, and Gorky into Chukchi. His books appeared in France, Finland, Italy, and Japan. The trilogy A Time of Melting Snows tells of the life and traditions of Chukotka.\par
Rasul Gamzatov was an Avar poet from a Dagestani aul, son of the people\u8217?s poet Gamzat Tsadasa. He published his first poem as a schoolboy. Two older brothers died in the war; a 1943 collection is dedicated to the heroism of soldiers. He studied at the Literary Institute. The song set to his poem \u8220?Cranes\u8221? became one of the best loved in the country: soldiers who did not return from the fields are imagined as white cranes. An epigraph to this theme is the Japanese story of a girl who folded paper cranes, hoping to recover from radiation sickness.\par
Sholem Aleichem (Solomon Rabinovich, 1859\u8211?1916) wrote in Yiddish; the pen name means \u8220?peace be upon you.\u8221? He was born into a poor Jewish family in Pereyaslav, tried many trades, but made literature and enlightenment his main work: he published the Jewish People\u8217?s Library and paid fees to beginners. He was compared to Mark Twain \u8212? and vice versa. Alexander Fadeyev said that Sholem Aleichem, having taken in the grief of the oppressed, never lost faith in something better.\par
Fyodor Volkov (1729\u8211?1763) is called the father of the Russian theater. In Yaroslavl, together with friends, he staged plays and wrote the music himself. Townspeople raised money for a wooden theater on the Volga bank (1751). Elizaveta Petrovna summoned the company to St. Petersburg and would not let them go home. In 1756 a decree opened the first Russian public theater \u8212? open not only to the court. Volkov refused ranks and remained a man of the stage. He caught a chill preparing a masquerade for the coronation of Catherine II in a damp Maslenitsa week and died at thirty-four. The Yaroslavl theater bears his name.\par
Gombojab Tsybikov (1873\u8211?1930) was a Buryat Orientalist, ethnologist, and photographer. He finished the gymnasium in Chita with a silver medal and the Oriental Faculty in St. Petersburg with a gold one. Tibet was then closed to foreigners, but an exception was made for a Buddhist from Transbaikalia. He spent 888 days there, kept notes in secret, hid his camera from strangers\u8217? eyes, and took about two hundred photographs. The book A Buddhist Pilgrim at the Shrines of Tibet (1919) was translated into many languages; the Russian Geographical Society awarded him the Przhevalsky Prize.\par
Dmitri Shostakovich was a twentieth-century composer. He took his love of music from his family, studied at the Petrograd Conservatory in the hungry 1920s, and earned money as a cinema pianist. His First Symphony, the diploma work of a twenty-year-old, at once went around halls in the USSR, Europe, and the United States. In blockaded Leningrad he put out incendiary bombs in a volunteer fire brigade and wrote the Seventh, the \u8220?Leningrad\u8221? Symphony. In August 1942 it was performed in the besieged city: musicians were recalled from the front, and the broadcast was heard on the radio. The American poet Carl Sandburg wrote that this music tells the world of a people who suffer and fight for the human spirit.\par
Galina Ulanova was a ballerina who had monuments put up to her in her lifetime in St. Petersburg and Stockholm. The rehearsal hall of the Bolshoi Theatre bears her name; a tulip was bred in her honor. Daughter of artists of the Mariinsky Theatre, from the age of nine she was in a theater school where the main word became \u8220?I must.\u8221? She met the war with performances in hospitals and on stage in Alma-Ata. From 1944 she was a soloist of the Bolshoi. Of fame she spoke calmly: it got in the way; cameras clicked from every side; she wanted to cover herself and leave. After leaving the stage she remained a coach-repetiteur to the end of her life.\par
Homework and a project in this section ask students to tell about a folk narrator, about the decorative applied art of any people of Russia, or to prepare a report on one of these names: Pushkin, Tolstoy, Levitan, Stanislavsky, Kapitsa, Gagarin, Konenkov, Nikulin, Plisetskaya, Botvinnik.\par
Figures canonized as Orthodox saints\par
A separate chapter shows that a contribution to culture is not only scientific or artistic. The Russian Church canonized people for service to the Fatherland and to the faith.\par
Alexander Nevsky (1221\u8211?1263) was prince of Novgorod, then of Kiev and Vladimir. He lost not a single battle: the Battle of the Neva in 1240 against the Swedes, the Battle on the Ice of 1242 on Lake Peipus against the German knights \u8212? he was then twenty-one. He repelled Lithuanian raids and negotiated with Batu to spare the land new devastations. He refused papal envoys who asked him to accept the \u8220?Latin faith.\u8221? His vita pictures him tall, with a voice \u8220?like a trumpet among the people.\u8221? He was canonized in 1547 as a pious prince. The textbook asks: can one speak of a contribution to culture if a person was not a poet and made no scientific discoveries?\par
Andrei Bogolyubsky (c. 1111\u8211?1174), son of Yuri Dolgoruky, moved the capital of the principality to Vladimir. He was called a church-builder: the Dormition Cathedral, the Golden Gates, the Church of the Intercession on the Nerl. He established the feasts of the Savior and of the Intercession and brought a wonder-working icon of the Mother of God to Vladimir. Boyars whose rights the prince had limited formed a conspiracy; he was killed in his own castle at Bogolyubovo. He was canonized around 1702. The textbook asks students to look long at the Church of the Intercession on the Nerl: silence, grace, peace, beauty.\par
Saint Gury of Kazan (c. 1500\u8211?1563) was born in Radonezh and served as steward to Prince Penkov. Envious people slandered him, and the young man was thrown into an earthen dungeon: he was fed oats, and water was given once every five days. A friend offered food \u8212? Gregory asked for ink and paper, wrote school copybooks, and gave the proceeds to the poor. Once free, he went to the Joseph-Volokolamsk Monastery, took the name Gury, and was abbot for nine years. In 1555 he became archbishop of Kazan and Sviyazhsk. He compared converting adult people of other faiths to a crooked tree that can no longer be straightened, so he gave his main attention to the young. In his time the Annunciation Cathedral, churches, and monasteries rose in Kazan, and a school was opened for Tatar children and other local peoples.\par
Nestor the Chronicler (c. 1056 \u8211? early twelfth century) was a monk of the Kiev Caves Monastery. The lives of Boris and Gleb and of Theodosius of the Caves are attributed to him, but his chief work is the Primary Chronicle: whence the Russian land came to be, and who first reigned in Kiev. This is not a list of dates but a meditation on the country\u8217?s fate and a call to unity. His sources were monastic records, tales, and the stories of elders, merchants, and travelers. Pushkin later took the chronicler\u8217?s image for Pimen in Boris Godunov. Nestor is canonized; his relics lie in the caves of the lavra. The textbook asks students to weigh his advice about books today: books water the universe with wisdom and teach restraint.\par
The moral culture of the person and of society\par
With this chapter a large block begins: \u8220?Moral values of the Russian people.\u8221? The moral culture of society is the norms, laws, traditions, and habitual relations among people at a given stage of history. The moral culture of a person is how far he or she has understood and applies those norms, can tell good from evil, and follows the \u8220?golden rule\u8221?: treat others as you wish to be treated.\par
A person is judged on four sides: what he knows about morality; what feelings a meeting with the good and the bad awakens; how he judges others\u8217? deeds and his own; how he behaves in fact. To this are added a culture of the mind (telling good from evil), a culture of feeling (sympathy), a culture of behavior, and outward etiquette.\par
Short plots by Leo Tolstoy and Valentina Oseyeva make the point. Two travelers meet a bear: one climbs a tree, the other pretends to be dead; the bear \u8220?whispers\u8221? in his ear that those who abandon a companion in trouble are bad. A siskin in a cage will not sing and asks for freedom. An old man plants apple trees not for himself \u8212? others will eat the fruit. A brother and sister forbid each other to touch their toys and soon grow bored apart. Two boys watch a dog attack a kitten and excuse themselves: \u8220?we weren\u8217?t doing anything\u8221? \u8212? a woman answers that that is the shame. Beside them sound Ossetian, Tatar, Buryat, and Dagestani proverbs about friendship, slander, and truth.\par
The chapter\u8217?s conclusion: the morality of a society is the morality of the people who live in it. Rules accumulated over millennia in labor, festival, and everyday converse. So ethics took shape \u8212? a code of manners and ways of living among others. Not to betray a friend, not to harm the living, to know how to agree, to live more for others than for oneself \u8212? such are the bearings the textbook offers.\par
Conscience, justice, and the intelligentsia\par
What inside us stops a bad deed? Conscience is an inner voice that wakes shame and makes one ask: did I lie, offend, turn away? Hence the expressions \u8220?conscience tormented me,\u8221? \u8220?a quiet conscience,\u8221? \u8220?to do something conscientiously,\u8221? \u8220?pangs of conscience.\u8221?\par
Justice is compared to scales: the pans are even \u8212? the deed is fair; a tilt means someone was wronged. A parable of the plantain: it is not taken for a bouquet because it is plain, yet it is the one that heals a scraped knee. A parable of the nettle and the rosebush: the bush gives flowers and receives care; the nettle only stings and wonders why it has a worse place. The meaning is the same: usefulness and kindness matter more than show.\par
The textbook separates the quality of the intelligentsia from a diploma. Dmitry Likhachev describes it as a living interest in knowledge and history, an aesthetic sense, the ability to understand another person and not show rudeness, envy, or gloating; one cannot pretend to have it. Vasily Shukshin adds: a rare joining of mind, an uneasy conscience, and compassion for the people\u8217?s fate. Chekhov thought bad speech as improper for such a person as not knowing how to read. \u8220?Spare\u8221? words are empty fillers \u8212? \u8220?well,\u8221? \u8220?you know,\u8221? \u8220?I mean,\u8221? \u8220?so to speak\u8221? \u8212? that clog thought; a comic poem by Emma Moshkovskaya mocks them.\par
A tale of the Wind, who could only make noise, leads to a simple thought: real work is not fuss for oneself but quiet help to another. At last the Wind rocks a cradle and soothes a child \u8212? and only then do his pranks become a meaning. From here the book turns to patriotism: sometimes a moral choice demands a sacrifice.\par
