Part II. Patriotism, humanism, religions, labor, intellect\par
From the second part of the scan (approximate pages 31\u8211?60)\par
Patriotism as a citizen\u8217?s moral feeling\par
Patriotism here is not a slogan but the readiness, in a hard hour, to choose the common cause rather than one\u8217?s own salvation. The peoples of Russia have more than once had to fight wars of liberation; 9 May, 23 February, and since 2007 9 December \u8212? Heroes of the Fatherland Day \u8212? recall this. Likhachev writes that love of the homeland is grown by love of home, school, and language; otherwise a person becomes like tumbleweed. Dobrolyubov again links patriotism to the wish to work for the country\u8217?s good.\par
The Battle of Kulikovo, 1380. When the Horde broke the forward regiment and the left flank, the ambush regiment of Prince Vladimir of Serpukhov and voivode Dmitry Bobrok Volynsky decided the outcome: a blow in the rear put Mamai\u8217?s army to flight. After this Prince Dmitry became Donskoy. On a high relief at the Donskoy Monastery, Sergius of Radonezh, who blessed the host, stands beside them.\par
The Patriotic War of 1812 is so called because people were defending not only an army but the home itself. Tolstoy wrote of the \u8220?club of the people\u8217?s war,\u8221? which fell on the invaders until the invasion perished. Beside regular troops fought Cossacks, militia, and partisans; among national regiments were Bashkir, Crimean Tatar, and Kalmyk ones; the peoples of the Volga region joined popular bands. Cavalry scouted, struck supply trains, and guarded villages from looters. At the Bagration fl\u232?ches at Borodino, Alexander Tuchkov, youngest of four brother-generals, took the banner and led his regiment in a counterattack; he was mortally wounded. Three of the brothers died in that war. Marina Tsvetaeva later recognized his fragile profile on a half-worn engraving.\par
The Great Patriotic War is shown as the common cause of many peoples. Among more than eleven thousand Heroes of the Soviet Union are Russians, Tatars, Jews, Mordvins, Chuvash, Bashkirs, Ossetians, Udmurts, Kalmyks, Kabardians, Adyghe, Abkhaz, and Yakuts. Alexander Matrosov at nineteen covered a pillbox embrasure near the village of Chernushki; hundreds of soldiers repeated a similar deed. Boris Tsarikov went into reconnaissance as a teenager after his family was killed and was among the first to cross the Dnieper. Zinaida Portnova of the Young Avengers distributed leaflets, took part in sabotage, poisoned an officers\u8217? mess; caught in an ambush, she shot the interrogator, betrayed no one, and was executed at seventeen. Students are also asked to learn about Alexei Maresyev, who returned to aviation after the amputation of his legs.\par
The Church in Rus more than once called people to defend the land. Priest Iov Kaminsky in 1829 went onto a Turkish rampart with a cross. Hieromonk Ioanniky Savinov at Sevastopol in 1855 sang prayers on the front line. By 1904 more than a hundred priests had been awarded a gold pectoral cross on the St. George ribbon. In 1941\u8211?1945 clergy were doctors, machine gunners, infantrymen; the Church collected about a hundred million rubles for the defense fund and built the Dmitry Donskoy tank column and squadrons named for Alexander Nevsky. Fyodor Puzanov, a St. George cavalier of the First World War, collected money for tanks under occupation and worked in reconnaissance. Archimandrite Nifont (Nikolai Glazov) fought as an anti-aircraft gunner, was left disabled after a wound at Lake Balaton, and took part in the Victory Parade. The Bishops\u8217? Council of 2000 called a Christian\u8217?s patriotism active: defense of the fatherland, labor for the country\u8217?s good, care for the people\u8217?s life and national culture.\par
In 1942 in Ufa the Muslim clergy addressed the faithful: there is no believer whose son, brother, or father would not be fighting the invaders; \u8220?love of the Homeland comes from faith\u8221? \u8212? words the address links with the Prophet Muhammad. Among Tatars there were 161 Heroes of the Soviet Union. Twice Hero Amet-Khan Sultan shot down about fifty aircraft, after the war tested equipment, including in weightlessness, preparing Gagarin\u8217?s flight, and died on a test. Musa Gareyev fought over Stalingrad, Crimea, Belorussia, Poland, and East Prussia.\par
A feat can happen in peacetime as well. Five-year-old Danila in Buryatia held with his teeth the hood of his sister, who had gone through the ice, until an older boy came. Fifteen-year-old Alexander Domanin in Krasnoyarsk Territory saved drowning girls and drowned himself; he was decorated posthumously. The other side is indifference: teenagers laugh at an old woman who has fallen and say \u8220?we weren\u8217?t doing anything.\u8221? The textbook returns again to Oseyeva\u8217?s thought: inaction is also a deed.\par
Humanism, mercy, and charity\par
The word \u8220?humanism\u8221? comes from the Latin for \u8220?humaneness.\u8221? A humane person holds every inhabitant of the planet to be of value: life, interests, rights, and freedoms must not be violated. Time and the wish to do good depend on the person.\par
Parables of the monk Barnabas show this. The virtues invite one for a walk \u8212? a hundred reasons appear to refuse; evil habits invite \u8212? at once there is time and strength. When Evil falls ill, no one notices; when Good falls ill, even those who did evil feel the emptiness. Three passers-by see a car in a ditch: one reproaches, the second is in a hurry, the third silently puts his shoulder to it. Compassion is not only help to the old and to children but the ability to hear another\u8217?s pain and not turn away.\par
Fifth-graders define mercy in different ways: to give alms; to put oneself in the sufferer\u8217?s place; to help in hope of thanks. The textbook sides with those who do not wait for a reward: mercy is sympathy without a bargain. Charity is the same feeling become a deed.\par
In Rus, care for the poor is linked with the adoption of Christianity: Prince Vladimir charged the clergy to look after the old, the sick, and the destitute; hospitals, refectories, and shelters appeared at monasteries. On the eve of feasts and victories tsars visited prisons and almshouses. In the seventeenth century Fyodor Alekseyevich ordered homes opened for homeless children, with teaching of literacy and a trade. Maria Fyodorovna, wife of Paul I, was called a \u8220?minister of charity\u8221?: she opened pedagogical classes and created an office of aid to invalids, orphans, and the elderly. Children are asked to invent kind deeds for Victory Day, International Nurses Day, Children\u8217?s Day, Older Persons\u8217? Day, Tolerance Day, and Persons with Disabilities Day \u8212? and projects on charity in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries and in Russia today.\par
Religion and morality in Russia\u8217?s traditional religions\par
Before speaking of religions the textbook recalls that even in prehistory behavior was held by taboos \u8212? strict bans (on eating a sacred animal, on marriage between close relatives, on cursing) \u8212? and by customs, that is, \u8220?this is how it is done among our people.\u8221? Religions did not invent morality from nothing: they gathered rules already living in society and made them a guide for the believer.\par
Orthodoxy, Islam, Buddhism, and Judaism are Russia\u8217?s traditional religions. In all of them goodness, justice, freedom and responsibility, conscience and duty stand at the center. Christian bearings are written in the Gospel: to overcome malice, to strive for perfection, to do good. The norms of Islam are in the Quran: to honor parents and elders, to care for children, to spare the living. Judaism rests on the Torah: justice, protection of widows and orphans, love of one\u8217?s neighbor. In Buddhism the moral law is given by the teaching of the Buddha, not by a command of God.\par
Orthodox priests in the textbook speak in simple words. Morality is a guidebook; the law is given by God and belongs to all people; conscience is an inner law and the voice of God. Good and bad are tested by one\u8217?s relation to parents and children, to the Homeland, to one\u8217?s neighbor. Faith that is reduced to ritual without love of the person is empty; one cannot lean on egoism and a thirst for gain. Patriarch Kirill reminds readers that the judgment of conscience already goes on in earthly life, and comfort makes a person weaker and more fearful. St. Ephrem the Syrian taught that one should occupy the mind with good so as not to think evil, and mocked those who love to teach but do not love to obey.\par
According to the Quran the most worthy is the one who is foremost in piety. Good is commanded toward parents, kin, orphans, the poor, the neighbor \u8212? both related and stranger \u8212? and the traveler. Allah does not love the proud and the stingy; He loves the patient. Do not judge in rage or in excessive joy; share even with the one who does not hold out a hand; be humble and just. Do not insult aging parents; ask the Lord to forgive them, because they raised you. One who is full while a neighbor is hungry is not a Muslim; faith is incomplete until you wish for your brother what you wish for yourself.\par
The chief commandment of Buddhism is compassion for all that lives. Well-being is in a person\u8217?s own hands: to be happy, one must make others happy. There are no trifles in conduct. Right speech is without lying, slander, and idle talk; right action is honest and peaceable; right livelihood is not to cause harm. The three chief sins are craving, pride, and egoism. Many monks are vegetarians; they strain water so as not to destroy even a tiny creature. Children are taught to live with animals; in some monasteries tigers live beside people. The elephant is wisdom, the dog devotion, the tiger strength, the lion an image of the Buddha, the snake wisdom (by legend it sheltered the Buddha from rain), the turtle luck. The \u8220?three monkeys\u8221? teach one not to see, hear, or speak evil.\par
Judaism is more than three thousand years old. The Exodus from Egypt is linked with the Sinai revelation to Moses: the Ten Commandments became a rule of life. Among them: honor your father and mother, do not kill, do not steal, do not bear false witness. Righteousness is tested by one\u8217?s relation to oneself (gentleness, simplicity), to people (justice, truth), and to God (the fear of God, refusal of evil). The section\u8217?s project is the symbols of Russia\u8217?s traditional religions.\par
Labor as a universal human value\par
Labor in the textbook is both social service and, in a religious view, a path of the soul\u8217?s salvation. Voltaire said that labor frees one from boredom, vice, and want; Gorky \u8212? that love of the work raises even rough labor to creation; Aesop called the ability to work a true treasure; the Talmud shames the one who is advised to learn diligence from the ant \u8212? and shames twice over if the advice is not taken.\par
A Christian parable of the peasant Terpigorev: hail, cattle plague, hay flooded, lightning burns the hut. He is ready to give up and go begging, but he sees a spider that twelve times weaves a torn web. \u8220?Patience and labor will grind everything down\u8221? \u8212? the man goes out to work, gathers a harvest, puts up a new little hut. An Eastern parable of two painters: the winner is not the one who sees no flaws in his picture, but the one who honestly knows which flaw to start with \u8212? he still has room to grow. A parable of the vine-grower warns: to take the supports from the vine and cut it back after a generous harvest is to have nothing in spring. Nature\u8217?s generosity must not be taken for granted.\par
The history of awards shows that the state has long marked not only a warrior\u8217?s feat but also work. Catherine II \u8212? medals \u8220?for labors useful to society\u8221? and the Order of St. Vladimir for zealous service; Alexander I \u8212? medals to merchants and peasants; Nicholas I \u8212? \u8220?for zeal\u8221?; Nicholas II \u8212? the Order of Princess Olga exclusively for women. In the USSR labor was called a duty and a matter of honor.\par
Mikhail Kalashnikov grew up in a large peasant family, commanded a tank in the war, was wounded, and in hospital began to design weapons. The 1947 assault rifle brought him a Stalin Prize; the design bureau created more than a hundred models. A doctor of technical sciences without a school certificate, a writer, Hero of Russia at ninety \u8212? the textbook gives these facts as an example of persistence. Dmitry Likhachev studied Old Russian literature at the Pushkin House for more than half a century, wrote dozens of books, headed the Cultural Foundation, spoke on radio and television; he was called the nation\u8217?s conscience. For him indifference to monuments of the past is indifference to the homeland, and the chief value of the world is life in all its forms, including the life of culture. Valentina Golubeva, an Ivanovo weaver, tended dozens of looms beyond the norm, fulfilled a five-year plan in a year and a half, then ran a mill and taught the young.\par
The conclusion: labor feeds the person and society, moves the economy, culture, and the school, and forms character. For conscientious work for the good of the Fatherland, orders and medals are given \u8212? from the Hammer and Sickle to the title Hero of Labor of the Russian Federation.\par
The intellectual culture of Russian society\par
The word \u8220?intellect\u8221? means cognition, understanding, reason. It is not a privilege of \u8220?mental labor\u8221?: the ability to solve problems is needed in any work. The intellectual culture of society is the sum of what people have learned about nature, society, and the human being: concepts, laws, texts, conclusions. A person\u8217?s usefulness is measured by the contribution of his thought, speech, and imagination to the common mind.\par
A diagram of personal intellectual culture includes sensation, perception, memory, attention, thinking, imagination; the ability to explain and to build an argument; comparison, analysis, synthesis; work with information; curiosity and independence; the ability to apply knowledge in new conditions.\par
The history of intellect is given as a chain of turning points. The first is when a human being understood that a stick and a stone could be made into tools, then joined materials, mastered fire and metal. Speech gave explanatory thinking: beside myth appeared an attempt to understand a law. Writing, print, and the division of physical and mental labor created people whose work was knowledge. Ancient Greece gave Archimedes, Pythagoras, Euclid, Hippocrates, Herodotus, Plato, and Aristotle.\par
Russian landmarks the textbook asks students to consider: the ship Oryol (1668) and the fleet of Peter I; a blast furnace in Tula (1630); the Cherepanovs\u8217? steam locomotive (1833); Mozhaisky\u8217?s flying machine (1882); the Yakovlev and Frese automobile (1896); Popov\u8217?s radio receiver (1895). Deineka\u8217?s painting Nikitka \u8212? about the daring of flight \u8212? is an occasion to talk about courage of thought.\par
Dmitri Mendeleev (1834\u8211?1907) finished the Main Pedagogical Institute with a gold medal, became a master at twenty-two, and a doctor in 1865. The periodic system not only ordered the known elements but left cells for future ones; in 1955 the 101st element was named mendelevium. The scientist himself was proud not of the table but of thousands of students in whom he \u8220?sowed a good seed.\u8221? Alexander Mozhaisky, a naval officer, spent his life working toward a flying machine: a model rose a few meters and fell; he did not live to finish a second specimen, but he left drawings and the experience of gliders.\par
A human being differs by speech and thought: ideas become economy, politics, art, school. For thought to work, society needs conditions for the development of each person\u8217?s abilities. Projects of the section are the space age, discoveries of Soviet physicians, pedagogy, and the birth of higher education in Russia.\par
