Part III. Aesthetic culture\par
From the third part of the scan (approximate pages 61\u8211?72)\par
Aesthetic values and the arts\par
The last section asks: what is aesthetic culture, what values it has, what kinds of art there are, and how to develop personal taste. First the children learn to name emotions from faces in photographs: anger, surprise, fear, delight, joy.\par
The saying \u8220?there is no accounting for taste\u8221? is only partly true. One person loves classical music, another jazz, one a landscape, another a history painting. But there are also shared ideas of the beautiful and the ugly. The aesthetic culture of society is the views and artistic values that citizens share; some of them have become universal. A child learns what to count as beautiful from family and surroundings, so ideals differ. The test is simple: if an object awakens a bright emotion, it is aesthetically valuable; if it repels, it is not. Any such object is the fruit of one person\u8217?s or many people\u8217?s creativity.\par
To live without art is possible only by impoverishing oneself. Literature acts by the word, music by sound, painting by color, ballet by movement. Art historians divide the arts into three groups. Spatial and plastic: the visual arts, decorative applied art, architecture, photography. Dynamic arts, which need time: music and literature. Spectacle arts: choreography, cinema, theater, circus.\par
Folk art and ornament\par
Folklore \u8212? bylinas, fairy tales, proverbs \u8212? enriches speech with images. A person whose talk is full of winged words is interesting to speak with. Decorative applied art makes things needed in daily life beautiful. Each people has its own means of expression: ornament, color, symmetry, form, rhythm. From a pattern one can often tell whose object it is.\par
The Tatars have three main types of ornament: floral (often a tulip motif), zoomorphic (ducks, birds), and geometric (waves and other figures). A fish in a pattern is a sign of wealth and plenty. A Nenets legend explains the ornament on kisy, footwear of reindeer hide, as a law of life given by the deity Num: a triangle is the chum, crosswise stripes are prohibitions, smoke, the sky, rays of the sun, neighboring chums around. Ancestors of the northern peoples counted patterns as amulets: they covered collar, cuffs, and hem \u8212? places through which, by belief, evil forces might enter. Images of the sun, moon, earth, fire, and reindeer kept misfortune at a distance.\par
Children are asked to choose a concert, a book of verse, a ballet, Levitan\u8217?s painting Golden Autumn, or a cinema hall and to say what value this kind of art carries and what impression it leaves. A separate talk is about their own tastes: what music they love, whether they go to the theater, whether they read verse, and whether taste needs to be developed.\par
Aesthetics born of religion: church, icon, mosque\par
Religion created not only moral rules but a special beauty. An Orthodox church is a work of architecture gathered over centuries. A chapel is small, often wooden, without an altar: the liturgy is not served there, but a moleben and a funeral service are possible; chapels are set by large cathedrals, in villages, by roads, in cemeteries. A church is the main building with an altar, a priest, and services; there are monastic, parish, and cemetery churches. A cathedral is the same church, but larger and more solemn. The Gospel words \u8220?where two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them\u8221? explain why people need this space.\par
Inside, the church is divided into the sanctuary, the nave, and the narthex. The sanctuary is \u8220?heaven on earth\u8221?; clergy enter there; in the center is the holy table, a four-sided table, the most sacred place. An iconostasis separates the sanctuary: in the middle the Royal Doors as the gates of paradise, at the sides the northern and southern doors. The narthex by the entrance is a place for those who still feel unready to go in, and a stall of candles and crosses.\par
An icon is a mediator between a person and the divine world, painting and prayer at once. The Kazan icon, by tradition, was revealed after a fire in 1579 to ten-year-old Matrona: the Mother of God pointed to a place in the ashes; the image was found about a meter down. The Vladimir icon was brought from Byzantium in the twelfth century; Andrei Bogolyubsky took it to Vladimir; it is called the protectress of the Russian land. In 1395, when Timur was marching on Moscow, Prince Vasily I ordered the icon brought from Vladimir; the city met it with prayer. The army stood at Yelets for two weeks and left without reaching Moscow. The Savior Not Made by Hands shows only the face of Christ \u8212? calm, without sharp feeling; since the seventeenth century the image has been linked with the Spasskaya Tower of the Kremlin. Projects are fresco, mosaic, and wood carving in Orthodox art.\par
A mosque carries another architectural language. The dome is a sign of the one God, the minaret a sign of the Prophet Muhammad. A courtyard with water and shaded halls give a sense of an immense and harmonious world. Islam does not permit depicting the Most High and living creatures, so the chief arts became calligraphy and the arabesque. To copy the word of the Quran beautifully is a deed that tradition promises to repay with paradise: the letter brings one nearer to God. An arabesque is an endlessly flowing geometric and vegetal pattern, polygons and many-pointed stars, contrasting colors from blue and emerald to red and yellow. The beauty of writing is read as purity of the inner world. Projects are the artistic symbols of Judaism, the image of the Buddha, Buddhist cult buildings.\par
The course in one closing thought\par
At the end the textbook asks not for a grade but for an honest look at oneself: was the study of this subject important, did knowledge widen, does one wish to continue in Grade 6. Review questions gather the whole path: what the spiritual culture of society and of the person is; under what conditions it grows; which moral qualities are chief (justice, patriotism, kindness, modesty, honesty \u8212? as against rancor and the mere ability to have one\u8217?s way); whose feats stayed in the memory; whether a breach of a norm must be followed by punishment; what the sources of culture are; what the intellect of a society depends on; what is brightest in the aesthetics of Orthodoxy; what arabesque and calligraphy mean.\par
If the three parts are folded into one sentence, it is this. Russia\u8217?s culture is a conversation of many peoples and several religions. People make it by labor, thought, conscience, and a sense of beauty. Patriotism is care for the common home, humanism is recognition of each person\u8217?s value, faith is a support of moral commandments, art is a way to see harmony in the world and in a holy place. A fifth-grader is asked not to memorize a list of names but to begin building a culture of his or her own: to think, to feel with others, to work, and not to pass by.\par
A short glossary of the retelling\par
At the end of the book there is a glossary. Below is not an extract from it but short explanations of the same circles of ideas, in other words.\par
Anarchy \u8212? a state in which there is no authority and no common order.\par
Archbishop \u8212? a senior bishop who oversees several church districts.\par
Piety \u8212? a life according to moral and religious rules.\par
Pilgrims \u8212? believers who go to venerated churches and shrines.\par
Vita (hagiography) \u8212? an account of a saint\u8217?s life.\par
Hegumen (abbot) \u8212? the superior of an Orthodox monastery.\par
Art history \u8212? the study of the origin and kinds of art.\par
Cult building \u8212? a building erected for worship.\par
Meshchane \u8212? in pre-revolutionary Russia, townspeople of modest means: craftsmen, small traders.\par
Novice \u8212? a person living in a monastery and preparing to become a monk.\par
Tonsure \u8212? the rite of entering monastic life.\par
Care of the needy (prizrenie) \u8212? care for the poor, the sick, and those who need guardianship.\par
Archpriest \u8212? a senior Orthodox priest.\par
Ordination \u8212? the conferring of holy orders.\par
Wanderer (strannik) \u8212? a traveler going on pilgrimage.\par
Taper \u8212? a musician who accompanies dances or silent film.\par
Erudite person \u8212? a person with wide knowledge in many fields.\par
Aesthetics \u8212? the teaching of the beautiful in art, nature, and life.\par
Ethnologist \u8212? a scholar who studies the life and history of peoples.\par
Holy fool (yurodivy) \u8212? in the Christian tradition, a person who has given up ordinary life and social proprieties.\par
